for more than 16 years glen carrot has  been providing fire and safety  management in the heart of Canada's oil  industry with three shops and dozens of  vehicles the business owner would  normally have 40 to 50 employees working  at oil and gas sites around Western  Canada but these days he's down to just  five or six workers while hundreds of  thousands of dollars of leased equipment  sits idle it's disheartening to have  people sitting at home they're phoning  me on a regular basis to find out if  we're going back to work and the bills  keep piling up and you know if we don't  have all this equipment out working it's  it's cost a lot of money every day every  day it's it's Canada's oil industry has  been reeling since late 2014 when crude  prices halfed from around $100 a barrel  to less than 50 that drop led to  bankruptcies and the loss of hundreds of  thousands of jobs in oil and gas a vital  sector in Canada's economy now with more  than three billion people locked down in  their homes because of the coronavirus  the demand for oil has evaporated prices  for Western Canadian select Alberta's  benchmark heavy oil have plummeted to  less than five dollars a barrel over the  past few weeks if prices don't rise  substantially the impact on oil workers  here could be devastating OPEX record  cut and oil output has failed to prop up  the price of crude and most Canadian  producers are now well below their  break-even point we have called it a  perfect storm and in many cases you know  the folks that I work with who have been  through many different cycles in this  industry quite frankly have never seen  anything quite like this and I don't  think anyone will quite possibly ever  see anything like this again Chad Miller  was laid off a few weeks ago and with  just 30 out of 515 oil rigs in operation  across the country he knows he's not  alone Miller hopes his Facebook group  oilfield dad's will help provide support  for those worried about how they'll pay  the rent or put food on the table it's  nothing worse than hearing about  somebody's family member you know I'm  turning to drugs or alcohol or even  suicide and it's  it touches all of us because it's dear  to our hearts that you know could be any  one of us last week the Canadian  government announced the 1.7 billion  dollar aid package to try to save  thousands of energy sector jobs but with  oil storage facilities already close to  capacity and demand at historic lows the  worst may be yet to come  David Mercer al Jazeera in central  Alberta Canada
 

